Gradle is a build automation tool using Groovy or Kotlin DSL instead of XML. Differences from Maven: 1) Configuration - Gradle uses build.gradle (Groovy/Kotlin) vs Maven's pom.xml, 2) Performance - Gradle faster with incremental builds, build cache, parallel execution, 3) Flexibility - Gradle more programmable, Maven convention-based, 4) Dependency resolution - both similar but Gradle has better conflict resolution. Example Gradle:
plugins {
    id 'java'
    id 'org.springframework.boot' version '3.2.0'
}

dependencies {
    implementation 'org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-web'
    testImplementation 'org.junit.jupiter:junit-jupiter'
}

Gradle advantages: faster builds, better for Android/multi-project builds. Maven advantages: simpler, more standardized, larger ecosystem. Choice depends on project needs - Gradle for performance/flexibility, Maven for simplicity/convention.

Gradle Java Library plugin provides implementation and api for dependency management. implementation: dependency not exposed to consumers (internal). api: dependency exposed to consumers (part of public API). Example:
// library-a/build.gradle
plugins {
    id 'java-library'
}

dependencies {
    api 'com.google.guava:guava:31.1-jre'  // Exposed to consumers
    implementation 'org.slf4j:slf4j-api:2.0.5'  // Internal only
}

// app/build.gradle
dependencies {
    implementation project(':library-a')
    // Can use Guava classes (api dependency)
    // Cannot use SLF4J classes (implementation dependency)
}

Benefits of implementation: faster builds (fewer recompilations), smaller API surface, encapsulation. Use api when: return types, method parameters, public fields use dependency. Use implementation when: internal usage only. Example: Library exposes public method return Guava ImmutableList → api. Library uses Jackson internally for JSON → implementation. Best practice: prefer implementation, use api only when necessary. Reduces coupling and improves build performance.

Publishing artifacts to private repository enables sharing across projects. Configuration for Nexus:

pom.xml:
<distributionManagement>
  <repository>
    <id>nexus-releases</id>
    <url>http://nexus.company.com/repository/maven-releases/</url>
  </repository>
  <snapshotRepository>
    <id>nexus-snapshots</id>
    <url>http://nexus.company.com/repository/maven-snapshots/</url>
  </snapshotRepository>
</distributionManagement>

~/.m2/settings.xml (credentials):
<settings>
  <servers>
    <server>
      <id>nexus-releases</id>
      <username>deploy-user</username>
      <password>{encrypted-password}</password>
    </server>
    <server>
      <id>nexus-snapshots</id>
      <username>deploy-user</username>
      <password>{encrypted-password}</password>
    </server>
  </servers>
</settings>

Deploy: mvn clean deploy. Releases deployed to releases repo, snapshots to snapshots repo. Encrypt passwords: mvn --encrypt-master-password and mvn --encrypt-password. CI/CD:
stage('Publish Artifact') {
  steps {
    configFileProvider([configFile(fileId: 'maven-settings', variable: 'MAVEN_SETTINGS')]) {
      sh 'mvn deploy -s $MAVEN_SETTINGS'
    }
  }
}

Gradle publishing:
publishing {
    publications {
        maven(MavenPublication) {
            from components.java
        }
    }
    repositories {
        maven {
            url = version.endsWith('SNAPSHOT') ? 
                'http://nexus/repository/maven-snapshots/' : 
                'http://nexus/repository/maven-releases/'
            credentials {
                username = project.findProperty('nexusUsername')
                password = project.findProperty('nexusPassword')
            }
        }
    }
}

Publish: gradle publish. Shared libraries published for consumption by microservices.

Gradle build cache stores task outputs for reuse across builds. When task inputs unchanged, Gradle reuses cached output instead of re-executing. Types: 1) Local build cache (default, ~/.gradle/caches/build-cache-1), 2) Remote build cache (shared across team/CI). Enable:
// gradle.properties
org.gradle.caching=true

// build.gradle
buildCache {
    local {
        enabled = true
    }
    remote(HttpBuildCache) {
        url = 'https://buildcache.company.com/'
        push = true  // CI pushes, developers pull
        credentials {
            username = findProperty('buildCacheUser')
            password = findProperty('buildCachePassword')
        }
    }
}

Task cacheability:
tasks.named('compileJava') {
    outputs.cacheIf { true }  // Task is cacheable
}

Benefits: Dramatically faster builds (80%+ time reduction for cache hits), shared cache across team (one person's build helps others), CI builds cached for developer use. Example: First build 10 min, cached build 1 min. Clean build with cache: downloads outputs, 2 min. Requirements: task inputs/outputs properly declared, deterministic tasks (same inputs → same outputs), no timestamp dependencies. Check cache effectiveness: gradle build --build-cache --info. Shows FROM-CACHE for cached tasks. Gradle 7+ has remote cache built-in. Essential for large projects.

Build failure management ensures quick feedback and resolution. Strategies:

1) Fast failure (fail early):
<plugin>
  <artifactId>maven-compiler-plugin</artifactId>
  <configuration>
    <failOnError>true</failOnError>
    <showWarnings>true</showWarnings>
    <compilerArgs>
      <arg>-Werror</arg>  <!-- Warnings as errors -->
    </compilerArgs>
  </configuration>
</plugin>

2) Parallel execution for faster feedback:
mvn -T 4 clean test  # 4 threads

3) Fail build on quality violations:
<plugin>
  <artifactId>maven-checkstyle-plugin</artifactId>
  <configuration>
    <failOnViolation>true</failOnViolation>
    <maxAllowedViolations>0</maxAllowedViolations>
  </configuration>
</plugin>

4) Jenkins notification:
post {
  failure {
    script {
      def failedStage = env.STAGE_NAME
      def buildLog = currentBuild.rawBuild.getLog(50).join('\n')

      slackSend(
        channel: '#build-failures',
        color: 'danger',
        message: """
          🚨 Build Failed
          Job: ${env.JOB_NAME}
          Build: #${env.BUILD_NUMBER}
          Stage: ${failedStage}
          Branch: ${env.GIT_BRANCH}
          Commit: ${env.GIT_COMMIT}
          Author: ${env.GIT_AUTHOR_NAME}
          URL: ${env.BUILD_URL}

          Last 50 log lines:
          ```${buildLog}```

          @${env.GIT_AUTHOR_NAME} please investigate
        """
      )

      emailext(
        to: '${GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL}',
        subject: "Build Failed: ${env.JOB_NAME} #${env.BUILD_NUMBER}",
        body: """
          Build failed at stage: ${failedStage}

          View console: ${env.BUILD_URL}console
          View tests: ${env.BUILD_URL}testReport
        """,
        attachLog: true
      )

      // Create Jira ticket for repeated failures
      if (currentBuild.getPreviousBuild()?.result == 'FAILURE') {
        sh '''
          curl -X POST jira-api/issue \
            -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
            -d '{
              "fields": {
                "project": {"key": "BUILD"},
                "summary": "Repeated build failure: '${env.JOB_NAME}'",
                "description": "Build has failed 2+ times. Investigate: '${env.BUILD_URL}'",
                "issuetype": {"name": "Bug"}
              }
            }'
        '''
      }
    }
  }
  fixed {
    slackSend(
      channel: '#build-failures',
      color: 'good',
      message: "✅ Build Fixed: ${env.JOB_NAME} #${env.BUILD_NUMBER}"
    )
  }
}

5) GitHub status checks:
stage('Update GitHub Status') {
  steps {
    script {
      def state = currentBuild.result == 'SUCCESS' ? 'success' : 'failure'
      sh """
        curl -X POST \
          -H "Authorization: token ${GITHUB_TOKEN}" \
          -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
          -d '{
            "state": "${state}",
            "target_url": "${env.BUILD_URL}",
            "description": "Build ${state}",
            "context": "jenkins/build"
          }' \
          https://api.github.com/repos/company/project/statuses/${env.GIT_COMMIT}
      """
    }
  }
}

6) Flaky test detection:
<plugin>
  <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
  <configuration>
    <rerunFailingTestsCount>2</rerunFailingTestsCount>
  </configuration>
</plugin>

7) Build metrics dashboard:
// Track failure rates
sh '''
  curl -X POST metrics-api/build \
    -d '{"job": "'${env.JOB_NAME}'", 
         "result": "'${currentBuild.result}'",
         "duration": '${currentBuild.duration}'}'
'''

Grafana dashboard shows: failure rate by project, average build time, most failed tests, builds by hour. Fast feedback + notifications ensure rapid fixes.

Build caching dramatically improves CI/CD performance. Strategies:

1) Maven - Cache local repository:
Jenkins:
pipeline {
  agent any
  options {
    buildDiscarder(logRotator(numToKeepStr: '10'))
  }
  stages {
    stage('Build') {
      steps {
        // Cache .m2 directory
        script {
          def m2Cache = "${env.WORKSPACE}/.m2"
          sh "mkdir -p ${m2Cache}"
          sh "mvn clean install -Dmaven.repo.local=${m2Cache}"
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

GitHub Actions:
- uses: actions/cache@v3
  with:
    path: ~/.m2/repository
    key: ${{ runner.os }}-maven-${{ hashFiles('**/pom.xml') }}
    restore-keys: |
      ${{ runner.os }}-maven-

GitLab CI:
cache:
  paths:
    - .m2/repository
  key: ${CI_COMMIT_REF_SLUG}

build:
  script:
    - mvn clean package -Dmaven.repo.local=$CI_PROJECT_DIR/.m2/repository

2) Gradle - Build cache:
gradle.properties:
org.gradle.caching=true
org.gradle.parallel=true
org.gradle.daemon=true

build.gradle:
buildCache {
  local {
    enabled = true
    directory = file("${rootDir}/.gradle/build-cache")
    removeUnusedEntriesAfterDays = 7
  }
  remote(HttpBuildCache) {
    url = 'https://gradle-cache.company.com/cache/'
    push = System.getenv('CI_COMMIT_BRANCH') == 'main'
    credentials {
      username = System.getenv('GRADLE_CACHE_USER')
      password = System.getenv('GRADLE_CACHE_PASSWORD')
    }
  }
}

GitHub Actions:
- uses: gradle/gradle-build-action@v2
  with:
    cache-read-only: ${{ github.ref != 'refs/heads/main' }}

3) Docker layer caching:
Jenkins:
pipeline {
  agent {
    docker {
      image 'maven:3.9-eclipse-temurin-17'
      args '-v $HOME/.m2:/root/.m2'  // Mount .m2 cache
    }
  }
  stages {
    stage('Build') {
      steps {
        sh 'mvn clean package'
      }
    }
  }
}

GitHub Actions with Docker:
- uses: docker/build-push-action@v4
  with:
    context: .
    cache-from: type=gha
    cache-to: type=gha,mode=max

4) Dependency pre-fetch:
stage('Prepare') {
  steps {
    sh 'mvn dependency:go-offline -B'
  }
}
stage('Build') {
  steps {
    sh 'mvn package -o'  // Offline mode
  }
}

5) Incremental builds (Maven):
mvn install -pl payment-service -am  # Only changed module + dependencies

Gradle:
gradle :payment-service:build --parallel

6) Cache invalidation strategy:
cache:
  key: maven-${{ hashFiles('**/pom.xml') }}-v2  # Version for manual invalidation
  paths:
    - ~/.m2/repository

7) Remote cache server (Gradle Enterprise):
Settings gradle:
plugins {
  id 'com.gradle.enterprise' version '3.15'
}

gradleEnterprise {
  buildScan {
    termsOfServiceUrl = 'https://gradle.com/terms-of-service'
    termsOfServiceAgree = 'yes'
  }
  buildCache {
    remote(gradleEnterprise.buildCache) {
      enabled = true
      push = System.getenv('CI') == 'true'
    }
  }
}

Results: Build time reduced from 15 min (cold) to 2 min (cached). Cache hit rate >80% for typical changes. Critical for large projects and frequent builds.
